# Graceful shutdown hands back the worker's own work before exiting

- `src/server/shutdown.ts`: `registerShutdownHandlers` now takes `{ startDraining(); hasInFlightWork(); releaseOwnedWork(): { runs; deliveries } }`, and `shutdown` calls `orchestrator.releaseOwnedWork()` on BOTH the clean and the timed-out path, inside `try { … } catch (e) { console.error("[radulf] releasing this worker's runs, deliveries and leases failed:", e); }`, before `process.exit(0)`.
- On success it logs `[radulf] interrupted N run(s) and M delivery/deliveries owned by this worker` (singular/plural handled, so `{ runs: 1, deliveries: 0 }` prints "interrupted 1 run and 0 deliveries owned by this worker").
- Header comment rewritten: the drain window's timeout no longer leans on `recover()`/the crash-recovery path — `releaseOwnedWork()` interrupts this worker's runs and review deliveries, frees its repo leases and deletes its `workers` row, so a replacement worker claims the card on its next pump instead of waiting out the stale-heartbeat reaper. A failure there is logged, never fatal to the exit.
- `src/server/shutdown.test.ts` (new): fake timers plus stubbed `process.exit`/`console.log`, an `install()` helper that grabs the single new SIGTERM listener and an `afterEach` that removes only listeners this file added; asserts the clean path drains → releases → exits 0 (release strictly before exit, ordering via `invocationCallOrder`) and that the busy worker is NOT released at 1 s but IS released, then exits 0, once the 30 s window elapses.
- Checks: `npx vitest run src/server/shutdown.test.ts src/server/boot.test.ts` → 2 files / 5 tests passed; `npx tsc --noEmit` and `npx eslint` on both files clean.